<details><summary>Reveal the diagonal proof</summary>
For row $i$, define $d_i=1-T_{ii}$. If row $i$ equaled $d$, equality at coordinate $i$ would force a bit to equal its complement. Thus the complemented diagonal differs from every row. See the [diagonal argument](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cantor%27s_diagonal_argument) for broader context.
</details>

## The cost moves into information

Exact loading of $n$ freely chosen bits needs at least $2^n$ distinguishable states. Move the slider and watch this finite lower bound grow.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{visualization:1}}

{{demo:0}}

<details><summary>Why exact recovery forces injectivity</summary>
If two bitstreams loaded into the same state, reading each coordinate from that common state would make all their bits equal. Therefore distinct streams require distinct states. The unrestricted infinite theorem is the same argument without truncation.
</details>

## What finite experiments leave unseen

